# Shell scripts are executed, never diffed as text. Git's autocrlf conversion
# would otherwise turn them into CRLF on Windows checkouts, and bash then fails
# with `$'\r': command not found`. Pinning them to LF makes the M25.6 `test:ops`
# gate reproducible regardless of each developer's core.autocrlf setting.
*.sh text eol=lf

# M27.0: documentation and manifests were being rewritten to CRLF on Windows
# checkouts, producing an `LF will be replaced by CRLF` warning on every
# `git status`. The churn was not cosmetic: during M26.0 a hand-normalised file
# lost the leading dash of a bash comment, which silently truncated a block.
#
# There is no technical reason to keep them CRLF. Neither type is executed by a
# shell, and normalising them keeps diffs limited to real content changes. *.md
# is pinned rather than left to core.autocrlf precisely because the runbook is
# reviewed in diffs during incidents.
*.md text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# M28.0: the same reasoning applies to Compose files and the environment
# template. A CRLF YAML is parsed differently by some Compose versions, and
# .env.example is diffed every time a variable is added, so a whole-file EOL
# change buries the one-line change that was actually being reviewed.
*.yml text eol=lf
.env.example text eol=lf
